= Climates of the World

A foundational reference entry for the materia medica. Each substance entry carries its own *Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet* section, in which traditional preparations are read as climatic-constitutional corrections. This entry sets out the classical frameworks themselves — Ayurvedic, 中醫, Greco-Roman humoral, with a brief note on Tibetan Sowa Rigpa — and catalogues the world's major climate types in those terms, so the substance entries can cite this frame rather than restate it.

The aim is to engage each tradition on its own terms (internal coherence, technical vocabulary, canonical sources) and then to note convergences and divergences honestly. Modern Köppen–Geiger labels appear only as anchors — useful for mapping a region to a class, not as the primary frame.

== Classical frameworks for climate

=== Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ and _deśa_

The Ayurvedic frame has two axes: *temporal* (_ṛtu_, season) and *spatial* (_deśa_, region/terrain). Together they specify the climatic load on the body at a given place and time.

*The six ṛtus.* Caraka divides the year into six two-month seasons, in two great half-years — the _ādāna-kāla_ (northern course / sun-uptake, _śiśira_–_vasanta_–_grīṣma_: late winter–spring–summer, in which the sun "takes away" the body's strength and moisture) and the _visarga-kāla_ (southern course / sun-release, _varṣā_–_śarad_–_hemanta_: monsoon–autumn–early winter, in which the sun returns moisture and strength). Each ṛtu has a doṣa cycle: _sañcaya_ (accumulation), _prakopa_ (aggravation), _praśama_ (pacification). Vāta accumulates in _grīṣma_, aggravates in _varṣā_, pacifies in _śarad_; Pitta accumulates in _varṣā_, aggravates in _śarad_, pacifies in _hemanta_; Kapha accumulates in _śiśira_, aggravates in _vasanta_, pacifies in _grīṣma_.#footnote[Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 6 (_tasyāśitīya_ / _ṛtucaryā_); Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 3 (_ṛtucaryā_); Aṣṭāṅga Saṅgraha, Sūtrasthāna 4. The _ādāna_ / _visarga_ split and the doṣa-ṛtu cycle are the standard locus classicus for Indian seasonal medicine.]

*The three deśas.* Caraka and Suśruta classify terrain into three types, each predisposing inhabitants to characteristic constitutions:
- *Jāṅgala* (जाङ्गल) — arid, sparse vegetation, sunny, low-water: hot-dry zones. Predisposes to Vāta-Pitta tendency, lighter and tougher physiques, fewer Kapha disorders.
- *Ānūpa* (आनूप) — marshy, abundant water, dense vegetation, humid: hot-humid and wet zones. Predisposes to Kapha-Pitta tendency, softer flesh, more Kapha and skin disorders.
- *Sādhāraṇa* (साधारण) — mixed/temperate, neither extreme: balanced constitution, fewer characteristic excesses.#footnote[Caraka Saṃhitā, Vimānasthāna 3 (_janapadodhvaṃsanīya_) and Sūtrasthāna 25; Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 35. The classification is treated as both ecological and constitutional — what the land favors, the bodies of its people will show.]

The dietetic principle is direct: in each ṛtu and deśa, favor foods of qualities *opposite* to the climatic load. Summer (hot-dry-Pitta) calls for cooling-moistening-sweet; monsoon (cool-damp-Vāta) for warming-light-dry; winter (cold-dry-Vāta) for warming-heavy-unctuous.

=== Chinese 五運六氣, 六淫, and 五方

The Chinese frame has three interlocking pieces.

*The 六淫 (six external excesses).* 風 (wind), 寒 (cold), 暑 (summer-heat), 濕 (damp), 燥 (dryness), 火 (fire) are the six pathogenic climatic energies. In moderation each is the normal seasonal climate; in excess or out of season each becomes pathogenic, invading the body via the surface and the channels. Each attaches to an organ-correspondence: 風→Liver (rises, moves), 寒→Kidney (contracts, deepens), 暑→Heart (scatters, depletes fluids), 濕→Spleen (sinks, obstructs), 燥→Lung (dries, contracts the surface), 火→Heart/Pericardium (consumes yin).#footnote[The 六淫 doctrine is threaded through the entire _Huang Di Nei Jing_; concentrated treatment in _Su Wen_ chs. 5 (_yin yang ying xiang da lun_ 陰陽應象大論), 39 (_ju tong lun_ 舉痛論), and the 五運六氣 chapters 66–74.]

*The 五方 (five directions).* From _Su Wen_ ch. 5, geography is read into the five-phase system:
- 東 (east): 風, 春, 木, 肝, 青, 酸 (sour)
- 南 (south): 暑/熱, 夏, 火, 心, 紅, 苦 (bitter)
- 中 (center): 濕, 長夏 (late summer), 土, 脾, 黃, 甘 (sweet)
- 西 (west): 燥, 秋, 金, 肺, 白, 辛 (pungent)
- 北 (north): 寒, 冬, 水, 腎, 黑, 鹹 (salty)

This is the canonical anchor: the climates around the central Yellow River plain set the prototypes from which other world climates are read. Note especially 長夏 — the *late-summer / damp-earth* season — which is the Chinese system's recognition of a distinct humid-transitional climatic period, not collapsed into summer or autumn proper.#footnote[_Su Wen_, ch. 5 (_yin yang ying xiang da lun_ 陰陽應象大論). The 長夏 placement and its 脾-土-濕 cluster: standard five-phase doctrine.]

*Regional therapeutics.* _Su Wen_ ch. 12 (_yi fa fang yi lun_ 異法方宜論) states explicitly that different climates call for different medical methods: stone-needling in the east, moxibustion in the cold north, drug therapy in the dry west, acupuncture in the humid south, exercise-and-massage in the central damp. Climate determines not just diet but the form of medicine itself.#footnote[_Su Wen_, ch. 12 (_yi fa fang yi lun_ 異法方宜論). The canonical statement that medical method is climate-dependent.]

=== Greco-Roman humoral

The four-element / four-quality / four-humor system pairs each element with two qualities (hot/cold + dry/wet) and each humor with an element, a season, a climate, and a temperament:
- *Yellow bile* (_chole_) — Fire; hot + dry; summer; hot-dry climates; *choleric* temperament.
- *Blood* (_haima_) — Air; hot + wet; spring; warm-wet climates; *sanguine* temperament.
- *Phlegm* (_phlegma_) — Water; cold + wet; winter; cold-wet climates; *phlegmatic* temperament.
- *Black bile* (_melan-chole_) — Earth; cold + dry; autumn; cold-dry climates; *melancholic* temperament.

The Hippocratic treatise _De aëre, aquis, locis_ (_On Airs, Waters, Places_) is the most explicit ancient Western statement of climatic-constitutional reasoning. It argues that climate, water, and topography shape both individual bodies and cultural temperaments — and famously contrasts the "soft, gentle" Asians of mild climates with the "hardy, courageous" Europeans of variable cold climates. Galen develops the framework in _De temperamentis_, treating temperament as a humoral-climatic equilibrium.#footnote[Hippocrates, _De aëre, aquis, locis_ (_On Airs, Waters, Places_), Loeb edition (W. H. S. Jones). Galen, _De temperamentis_ — Singer translation (Cambridge, 1997); broader Galenic dietetics in _De alimentorum facultatibus_ (Powell, Cambridge). The Greco-Roman frame is climatically *Mediterranean-centered*; extrapolation to tropics, high altitudes, and polar climates is incomplete in the canon and is flagged accordingly in those climate-type sections below.]

=== Tibetan Sowa Rigpa (brief note)

The Tibetan medical canon — _rGyud bzhi_ (རྒྱུད་བཞི་), the Four Tantras, attributed to Yutok Yontan Gonpo (12th c.) — inherits the Ayurvedic frame but adapts it for the cold-dry high-altitude plateau: a uniquely strong treatment of cold-induced disorders, the _rlung_ (~Vāta) profile of plateau bodies, and the central role of butter and salt in plateau dietetics. For *any* cold-dry high-altitude climate (the Andes, the Ethiopian highlands, the Pamir), Sowa Rigpa is often the most directly applicable classical frame — a point worth holding when reading non-Tibetan high-altitude foods.#footnote[_rGyud bzhi_ (རྒྱུད་བཞི་) — verify against modern critical translations: Clark, _The Quintessence Tantras of Tibetan Medicine_; Yang Ga's recent scholarly work. The claim that Sowa Rigpa is the most directly applicable classical frame for non-Tibetan high-altitude climates is interpretive — flagged.]

== Climate types of the world

For each type: geographic range and Köppen–Geiger anchor → dravyaguna reading → 中醫 reading → Greco-Roman humoral reading → exemplary diet and constitutional tendencies. Read across the systems for convergences; respect divergences where the systems genuinely disagree.

=== Hot-arid (desert)

*Range:* Sahara, Arabian peninsula, Iranian plateau, Thar (western India), Atacama (Chile), central Australian outback, American Southwest. *Köppen:* BWh (hot desert).

*Dravyaguna.* The paradigm _jāṅgala_: extreme _rūkṣa_ (dry), _uṣṇa_ (hot), _laghu_ (light), _cala_ (mobile). Aggravates *Vāta* (on _rūkṣa-laghu-cala_) and *Pitta* (on _uṣṇa_); predisposes to fluid depletion (_rasakṣaya_), constipation, joint and skin dryness, sharp-tempered constitution. Bodies typically lean and sinewy.

*中醫.* 燥 (dryness) and 暑/熱 (heat) dominate; 風 in windy expanses. 五方: 西 (west — dryness, lung, autumn). Organs loaded: 肺 (Lung) by the dryness, 心 (Heart) by the heat, 腎 (Kidney) depleted by fluid loss.

*Greco-Roman.* Hot + Dry quadrant; *choleric* over the short term, *melancholic* over the long. The Hippocratic _Airs_ note on dry-hot climates producing "lean, sinewy, well-articulated" bodies fits the bedouin and saharan constitutional type.

*Exemplary diet.* Dates, milk and yogurt, ghee and clarified butter, slow-cooked grain (couscous, freekeh, bulgur), lentils with oil, mint and fennel teas, rose-water and orange-blossom waters, abundant salt for sweat losses. Levantine and Maghrebi cuisines are dense in *cooling-moistening-snigdha* preparations — yogurt sauces, tahini, olive oil, slow-stewed lamb with dried fruit — all climatically pointed.#footnote[Caraka's _jāṅgala_: Vim. 3 and Sū. 25. Dietary observations from standard regional ethnographies (Roden, _A Book of Middle Eastern Food_; Wolfert, _The Food of Morocco_), read against the dravyaguna frame.]

=== Mediterranean (warm-dry summer / cool-wet winter)

*Range:* Mediterranean basin proper, California, central Chile, southwest Australia, Western Cape of South Africa. *Köppen:* Csa (hot-summer), Csb (warm-summer).

*Dravyaguna.* A strongly *seasonal* climate. _Grīṣma_ and _śarad_ lean Pitta-Vāta; _hemanta_–_śiśira_ goes Vāta with damp; _vasanta_ is the canonical Kapha-aggravating season. Closer to _sādhāraṇa_ on average, with seasonal swings.

*中醫.* Summer 暑 + 燥; winter 寒 + 濕; spring 風. 五方: 西 (autumn dryness) and 南 (summer heat) anchored; 中 (damp earth) for olive-vine soils. Organs loaded seasonally: 肺 in summer dryness, 脾 in spring damp, 腎 in winter cold.

*Greco-Roman.* The canonical *home* of the humoral system — the four-season / four-humor cycle is most cleanly applicable here, since the system was developed in this climate. Hot-dry summer (choleric); warm-wet spring (sanguine); cool-wet winter (phlegmatic); cool-dry autumn (melancholic).

*Exemplary diet.* The Mediterranean diet — olive oil, bread, wine, fish, legumes, sheep and goat dairy, abundant fresh vegetables, citrus, fresh herbs (oregano, basil, rosemary, thyme). Long Hippocratic emphasis on *barley* (cooling), wine (warming), olive oil (moistening); the canonical balance against summer-heat / winter-cold.#footnote[Mediterranean dietetic frame: Hippocrates, _Regimen_ (Loeb, Jones); Davidson, _The Oxford Companion to Food_, "Mediterranean" entries.]

=== Hot-humid (tropical monsoonal / equatorial)

*Range:* South India (esp. Kerala), Sri Lanka, Indonesia, Philippines, southern Thailand, Vietnam, equatorial Africa, Amazon, the Caribbean, northern Australia. *Köppen:* Af (tropical rainforest), Am (tropical monsoon), Aw (tropical savannah).

*Dravyaguna.* The paradigm _ānūpa_: _guru_ (heavy), _kleda_ (sticky-moist), _uṣṇa_ (warm), _snigdha_-by-ambient-moisture. Aggravates *Kapha* (on _guru-kleda-snigdha_) and *Pitta* (on _uṣṇa_); *swings sharply into Vāta aggravation in _varṣā_* (monsoon) due to barometric, thermal, and digestive depression — the monsoon-Vāta point is fundamental to Indian climate-medicine and easily missed elsewhere. Predisposes to skin disorders (_kuṣṭha_), digestive sluggishness, fevers, parasitic disease. Bodies typically soft, well-fleshed, with strong sweat function but vulnerable digestion.

*中醫.* 濕熱 (damp-heat) dominates; 暑 in dry season. 五方: 南 + 中 jointly. Organs loaded: 脾 (Spleen) for the damp, 心 (Heart) for the heat. The 濕熱 disease category — concentrated in southern Chinese medical literature and most fully developed in the Ming-Qing *wen bing* (温病, warm-pathogen) tradition — was articulated in exactly this climate band.

*Greco-Roman.* The system extrapolates uneasily here — hot+wet is technically the *sanguine* / spring quadrant, but the Mediterranean four-season model has no genuine tropical category. Treat as outside the canonical Greco-Roman frame.

*Exemplary diet.* South Indian and Southeast Asian cuisines read as pharmacopoeias against damp-heat: bitter and pungent flavors (turmeric, asafoetida, mustard, ginger, pepper, chili), fermented sour foods (idli, dosa, fermented fish sauces, tempeh, kimchi-analogues), rice porridges, light moong/mūng dāl, coconut for cooling, lime and tamarind as sour-pacifying agents. Heavy ghee preparations are reserved for cool/dry season or used sparingly. _Pañcakola_-type carminatives — pippalī, pippalimūla, cavya, citraka, śuṇṭhī — are the classical Indian climatic correctives.#footnote[_Ānūpa_: Caraka Vim. 3. Damp-heat in Chinese medicine: Ye Tianshi, _Wen Re Lun_; modern: Liu Guohui, _Warm Pathogen Diseases_ (Eastland). South Indian and SE Asian climatic dietetics: Achaya, _Indian Food: A Historical Companion_; regional cuisine histories.]

=== Subtropical-humid

*Range:* Southeastern China (Hunan, Guangdong, Fujian, Taiwan), southeastern United States, eastern Australia, southern Japan (Kyushu, Okinawa), southern Brazil and Argentine pampas, eastern Mexican Gulf. *Köppen:* Cfa (humid subtropical).

*Dravyaguna.* Less extreme than tropical proper, but hot-humid summers are strongly _ānūpa_; mild winters with damp. Aggravates Kapha-Pitta; less monsoon-Vāta swing than tropical proper.

*中醫.* Summer 濕 + 熱; mild 寒 in winter. The classical southern-China *wen bing* literature was developed here. 五方: 南-中 hybrid.

*Greco-Roman.* Sanguine-leaning in summer; less directly addressed.

*Exemplary diet.* Cantonese cuisine — light steaming and blanching, soup culture, congee, bitter melon, herbal cooling teas (涼茶 _liáng chá_), ginger and scallion as warming correctives; southern US cuisine adds fermentation (sourdough, pickles), corn, beans, greens — all working against the damp-heat pattern, though without the explicit classical articulation.#footnote[Cantonese 涼茶: Anderson, _The Food of China_. Southern US adaptive features are observed pattern rather than classically-articulated dietetics — flagged.]

=== Cool-damp temperate (Atlantic / oceanic)

*Range:* Britain and Ireland, Brittany and Normandy, Atlantic Iberia, the Low Countries, Pacific Northwest of North America, southern Chile, southern New Zealand, Tasmania. *Köppen:* Cfb (oceanic).

*Dravyaguna.* Cool, damp, low-sun, wind-exposed — a *Kapha-Vāta* climate. Constitutional tendency: chronic _agni_ load, damp accumulation, melancholic-phlegmatic temperament classically observed.

*中醫.* 寒濕 (cold-damp) dominates. 五方: 中 (damp) + 北 (cold) hybrid. Organs loaded: 脾 (Spleen) primarily; 腎 (Kidney) in winter; 肺 (Lung) for chronic damp in the chest.

*Greco-Roman.* Cold + Wet — *phlegmatic*. Galen and the medieval European humoralists explicitly describe northern peoples as phlegmatic and ruddy from this climate.

*Exemplary diet.* Long-cooked grains (oat porridge, barley broth), root vegetables (turnip, potato, parsnip, swede), fermented dairy (butter, hard cheeses, cultured cream), salted and smoked meats and fish (kippers, gravlax, ham, bacon), fermented beverages (beer, cider), warming spices in winter baking (clove, cinnamon, nutmeg from imports, native juniper). The Scottish, Irish, and Dutch dietary patterns are textbook 寒濕-corrective. See `barley.typ` and `oats.typ` for detailed treatments.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme

*Range:* Northern China and Manchuria, Korea, central and eastern European Russia, the American and Canadian Midwest, the Pampas. *Köppen:* Dfa, Dfb (humid continental).

*Dravyaguna.* Wide seasonal swing: hot-humid summer aggravates Pitta-Kapha; cold-dry winter aggravates Vāta strongly. Two distinct constitutional pressures across the year.

*中醫.* Summer 暑濕; winter 寒燥. 五方: mixed 北-中-南 across seasons. The system's seasonal cycle is fully exercised here.

*Greco-Roman.* The full four-season cycle approximately applicable; canonical frame fits this climate better than tropics or high-altitude.

*Exemplary diet.* Northern Chinese cuisine: hearty wheat-based foods (steamed bread, noodles, dumplings), garlic and Welsh onion (蔥) and ginger as warming carminatives, fermented vegetables (酸菜, kimchi, sauerkraut), preserved meats, hot stews in winter; lighter and more sour preparations in summer. Korean cuisine's kimchi-and-bone-broth pattern is the same logic; Russian cuisine's cabbage-soup-and-pickle pattern is its Western parallel. The seasonal _kāla_-logic — eating winter forms in winter, summer forms in summer — is sharply enforced by the climate itself, and is the textbook setting for _kāla-viruddha_ (Caraka Sū. 26).#footnote[Northern Chinese seasonal dietetics: Anderson, _The Food of China_; Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_. Korean and Russian patterns: standard regional ethnographies.]

=== Cold-dry continental (steppe)

*Range:* Mongolian steppe, Anatolian plateau, Iranian plateau interior, American high plains, inner Central Asia. *Köppen:* BSk (cold steppe), BWk (cold desert) at the dry end.

*Dravyaguna.* Cold + dry, with windswept openness adding _cala_ to the _rūkṣa-laghu_ profile. A strong *Vāta* climate. Closer to _jāṅgala_ than _sādhāraṇa_, with cold as the distinctive component.

*中醫.* 寒 + 燥 dominate, with 風 from the open steppe. 五方: 北 with western dryness elements. Organs loaded: 腎 by the cold, 肺 by the dryness.

*Greco-Roman.* Cold + Dry — *melancholic* quadrant. Less canonically discussed but inferable.

*Exemplary diet.* Heavy reliance on dairy (yogurt, kefir, butter, fermented mare's milk _kumis_), meat (mutton, horse, beef), salted-buttered tea (Mongolian _suutei tsai_), grain in moderation (barley, millet, modern wheat). The dairy-heavy pattern provides _snigdha_ against the dryness; animal protein supplies internal heat against the cold; salt and tea are constitutive. The steppe _suutei tsai_ pattern is the lowland analogue of Tibetan po cha.#footnote[Mongolian and Central Asian dietetic patterns: Anderson, _Everyone Eats_; Allsen, _Food and the Mongols_. The _suutei tsai_ / po cha analogy is descriptive, not classical.]

=== Cold-dry high-altitude

*Range:* Tibetan plateau, Ladakh, the Andean altiplano (Bolivia, Peru), Ethiopian highlands, Pamir, Hindu Kush. *Köppen:* H (highland) — treated as a modifier rather than a clean class.

*Dravyaguna.* The most extreme *Vāta* climate of all: thin air, low oxygen, cold, dry, intense UV, wide diurnal temperature swing. Aggravates Vāta on every parameter; Kapha-low and Pitta-deplete tendencies emerge over time. Bodies typically wiry, with high red-cell adaptation; sustained altitude exposure produces a recognizable constitutional type.

*中醫.* 寒 + 燥 + 風 dominate; the high-altitude oxygen vacuum produces a constitutional 氣虛 (qi vacuity) component. 五方: 北 dominant. Organs loaded: 腎 (Kidney) and 肺 (Lung) jointly — the lung work of altitude is constant.

*Greco-Roman.* The system extrapolates uneasily — cold-dry maps to melancholic, but altitude is not a canonical parameter. Outside the Greco-Roman frame.

*Tibetan Sowa Rigpa.* This is the canonical *home* climate of the Tibetan tradition; the _rGyud bzhi_ dietetic chapters effectively treat plateau bodies as the default case. For any cold-dry high-altitude climate, Sowa Rigpa is the most directly applicable classical frame.

*Exemplary diet.* Roasted-grain staples (tsampa from naked barley in Tibet; quinoa, kañiwa, and freeze-dried _chuño_ in the Andes; injera-teff in Ethiopia), heavy dairy fat (yak butter on the plateau; alpaca and llama milk on the altiplano; clarified butter and milk in Ethiopia), salt (constitutive — supports Kidney-yang and replaces sweat losses), warming drinks (po cha; coca leaf infusions in the Andes; spiced butter tea analogues). See `barley.typ` for detailed tsampa + po cha treatment.#footnote[Andean and Ethiopian high-altitude dietetics: standard regional ethnographies (Murra, _Formaciones económicas y políticas del mundo andino_; literature on Ethiopian highland nutrition). The claim that Sowa Rigpa is the most directly applicable frame for non-Tibetan high-altitude is interpretive — flagged.]

=== Cold-damp boreal / subarctic

*Range:* Scandinavia, Russia north of Moscow, Canadian boreal, Alaskan interior, the Baltic states. *Köppen:* Dfc (subarctic continental), often with strong winter damp.

*Dravyaguna.* Cold + damp: Vāta-Kapha layered. Vāta from cold dry winter wind and short days; Kapha from chronic damp, snow, marsh. Heavy bodies, slow digestion, melancholic-phlegmatic temperament classically inferred. Wide seasonal swing into brief, intense summer Vāta-aggravation.

*中醫.* 寒濕 (cold-damp) dominates; the long winter produces 腎陽虛 (Kidney-yang vacuity) tendency. 五方: 北 with damp-earth admixture.

*Greco-Roman.* Cold + Wet — *phlegmatic*. The Galenic medieval European tradition explicitly addresses this climate.

*Exemplary diet.* Heavy use of dairy fat (butter, cream, sour cream), salted and fermented fish (lutefisk, gravlax, surströmming, rakfisk), root cellars (turnip, beet, cabbage, potato), rye and barley breads, fermented vegetables (sauerkraut, sour cabbage), warming spices in winter baking (cardamom in Nordic, cinnamon and clove in Russian), spirits as digestive warmers (akvavit, vodka). Heavy reliance on smoking, drying, and fermentation produces classical _saṃskāra_ corrections (cool raw → warming finished). See `oats.typ` for detailed Nordic treatment.#footnote[Nordic dietary patterns: Notaker, _A History of Cookbooks_; Davidson, _The Oxford Companion to Food_, Nordic entries.]

=== Polar / arctic

*Range:* Greenland, Inuit Arctic (northern Canada, northern Alaska, Chukotka), northern Sami areas, modern Antarctic research stations. *Köppen:* ET (tundra), EF (ice cap).

*Dravyaguna.* Extreme cold-dry-Vāta, with sustained winter darkness as an additional constitutional load. Classical Indian texts do not directly treat this climate; reasoning is by extension from cold-dry-high-altitude with darkness as an extra factor. Bodies famously adapted: compact, well-insulated, high basal metabolism.

*中醫.* Extreme 寒, with chronic yang-vacuum tendency. The classical Chinese frame does not directly treat polar; reasoning by extension from the northern-extreme pole.

*Greco-Roman.* Cold-extreme; outside the canonical frame.

*Exemplary diet.* The classical Inuit diet — almost entirely animal-based: seal, whale, fish, caribou, berries when available, fermented meats (_kiviak_), with fat as the dominant macronutrient (commonly >60% of calories). A dietary extreme made constitutionally necessary by the climate; classical Indian and Chinese dietetics have no canonical polar treatment — flagged as outside their frames.#footnote[Inuit diet: standard arctic nutrition literature (Kuhnlein and Receveur, _Annual Review of Nutrition_ 1996, on traditional food systems of indigenous peoples). The lack of canonical Indian/Chinese polar treatment is straightforward but worth flagging.]

=== Tropical highland

*Range:* East African highlands (Nairobi, Addis Ababa, Kigali), Andean cities (Quito, Bogotá, La Paz), Mexican highlands (Mexico City), parts of the New Guinea highlands. *Köppen:* Cwb, Cfb at altitude, sometimes H modifier.

*Dravyaguna.* A hybrid climate: cool-mild year-round, but high-altitude dryness, wind, and intense UV. Reads as Vāta-dominant with mild Pitta and low Kapha; the absence of strong seasonal swing makes the climate distinctive — neither truly _jāṅgala_ nor _sādhāraṇa_, sometimes treated as a *perpetual gentle autumn*.

*中醫.* Mild 寒 + 燥; low 暑 even at low latitudes due to altitude. 五方: 西-北 hybrid.

*Greco-Roman.* No clean treatment; closest match is cool-dry / melancholic.

*Exemplary diet.* Andean: potato in dozens of varieties, quinoa, kañiwa, _chuño_, lamb/alpaca/cuy, coca leaf as digestive and altitude adjunct, hearty soups. East African highland: injera-and-wat (Ethiopia: teff fermentation + spiced stews + clarified butter), maize and beans, fermented dairy (Ethiopian _ayib_, Kenyan _maziwa lala_), warming spices (Ethiopian _berbere_, _mitmita_). Mexican highland: maize, beans, chili, nopales, masa preparations. All read as moderate-warming-grounding climatic correctives.#footnote[Andean and East African highland nutrition: regional ethnographies. Berbere/mitmita as climatic correctives: interpretive reading — flagged.]

== Cross-system convergences and divergences

*Convergences.*
- All three classical systems treat *hot-dry* climates as drying-and-heating and call for cooling-moistening corrections.
- All three treat *cold-damp* climates as cold-damp-burdening and call for warming-drying corrections, often via fermentation and heating spices.
- All three recognize that climate shapes constitution at the population level — Hippocrates' _Airs, Waters, Places_, Caraka's _deśa_ doctrine, and _Su Wen_ ch. 12's regional-medicine doctrine make this point independently and explicitly.

*Divergences.*
- *Tropics.* Indian and Chinese medicine have rich, native treatments of tropical hot-humid climates (Caraka's _ānūpa_; Ming-Qing _wen bing_ literature). The Greco-Roman humoral system has no canonical tropical category and extrapolates poorly.
- *High altitude.* Sowa Rigpa is the canonical frame; Indian and Chinese systems handle altitude by extension; Greco-Roman has no treatment.
- *Polar.* Outside the canonical reach of all three classical systems; reasoning is by extension only.
- *Monsoon-Vāta.* The Indian system uniquely identifies the monsoon as a Vāta-aggravating season *despite* its dampness, on the strength of barometric and thermal swings. The Chinese 濕 frame treats wet seasons primarily as Spleen-loading; the Indian frame's Vāta-monsoon insight is distinctive and should be preserved when reading South Asian climates.
- *Warming-and-moistening grain.* The Greco-Roman humoral system classifies wheat as warming-and-moistening, while Indian and Chinese systems classify it as cooling. A real cross-system divergence — do not smooth over (see `wheat.typ`).

== Use within the materia medica

Each substance entry's *Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet* section should cite this entry as the foundational frame and treat only the climates relevant to its own cultivation/consumption. The per-substance sections *apply* the framework; they should not re-derive it. Where a climate falls outside one of the classical frames (high altitude in the Greco-Roman case; polar in all three), the substance entry should say so and treat that climate by extension, flagged. The per-substance *Modern transposition* paragraphs apply _deśa-viruddha_ (place) and _kāla-viruddha_ (season) per Caraka Sū. 26 — both standard cross-references to this entry's framework section.

